//? Title: Adaptive Optimizers: Adagrad to Yogi, Step Bounds and Counterexamples
//? Subtitle: Adagrad, RMSProp, Adam, AdamW, Nadam, AMSGrad and Adamax checked against kernel formulas, plus RAdam, AdaBelief and Yogi; the exact worst-case Adam step; RAdam's rectification against Monte Carlo; Reddi et al.'s counterexample; and a learning-rate sweep on digits, using datamunge's nn::Optimizer, nn::AdaptiveOptimizer, nn::radam_rectification and nn::adam_step_bound
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Adaptive optimizers divide each coordinate's step by a running estimate of its gradient scale. This report checks seven built-in rules against TensorFlow's kernel formulas and their first updates against closed forms. It adds three newer rules, RAdam, AdaBelief and Yogi, which agree with hand-written references to \(10^{-12}\).
A Cauchy–Schwarz argument gives the exact largest Adam step. With the default \(\beta_1=0.9\), \(\beta_2=0.999\) it grows to 7.27 learning rates, more than twice the value 3.16 usually quoted. Adam reaches it exactly on the extremal gradient history.
RAdam's rectification term tracks the Monte Carlo ratio it approximates to within 7% from step 20 on, and is more conservative in the first steps. On Reddi, Kale and Kumar's online problem, Adam and RMSProp converge to the worst point, while AMSGrad, Yogi and Adagrad find the optimum. AdaBelief with \(\beta_1=0\) cycles with exactly the regret 4/3 that its vanishing "belief" term predicts.
On the digits data, all ten rules reach 96–97% validation accuracy at their best learning rate. They differ in speed and sensitivity to the learning rate, not in final accuracy.
)

\toc

> The problem

Plain gradient descent uses one learning rate for every coordinate. When gradients differ in scale across coordinates or over time, that rate is too large for some and too small for others. Adaptive methods divide each coordinate's step by a running estimate of the gradient's magnitude:
- Adagrad uses the sum of squares (Duchi, Hazan and Singer, 2011);
- RMSProp uses an exponential average (Tieleman and Hinton, 2012);
- Adam adds momentum and bias correction (Kingma and Ba, 2015).

Many variants followed. Some fix a flaw in Adam's theory (AMSGrad; Reddi, Kale and Kumar, 2018). Others decouple weight decay (AdamW; Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019), change the momentum (Nadam; Dozat, 2016) or the denominator (Adamax; Yogi, Zaheer et al., 2018; AdaBelief, Zhuang et al., 2020), or replace learning-rate warm-up (RAdam; Liu et al., 2020).

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.3_optimizers_and_training_loop/adaptive_optimizers.cpp`. That example compared Adagrad, RMSProp and Adam with TensorFlow's fused-kernel formulas and checked the first update of all seven built-in rules, AMSGrad's envelope, Adamax's decay and AdamW's decoupled decay. It then fitted an anisotropic quadratic with each rule. All of that is kept.
Added are RAdam, AdaBelief and Yogi with their analysis functions, in `datamunge/nn/adaptive_variants.hpp`, together with an exact bound on Adam's step, checks of RAdam's and Reddi et al.'s theory, and a dataset.

> The method

>> The Adam family

For a gradient \(g_t\), Adam keeps \(m_t=\beta_1m_{t-1}+(1-\beta_1)g_t\) and \(v_t=\beta_2v_{t-1}+(1-\beta_2)g_t^2\). It corrects their bias towards the zero start and steps

$$
\theta_t=\theta_{t-1}-\eta\,\frac{\hat m_t}{\sqrt{\hat v_t}+\epsilon},\qquad \hat m_t=\frac{m_t}{1-\beta_1^t},\quad \hat v_t=\frac{v_t}{1-\beta_2^t}.
$$
\alttext(theta t equals theta t minus 1 minus eta times m hat t over the square root of v hat t plus epsilon, where m hat and v hat are m t and v t divided by 1 minus beta 1 to the t and 1 minus beta 2 to the t.)

On the first step \(\hat m_1/\sqrt{\hat v_1}=\mathrm{sign}(g_1)\), so every coordinate moves by \(\eta\). The variants change one ingredient each:
- AdamW subtracts \(\eta\lambda\theta\) separately;
- AMSGrad divides by the running maximum of \(v_t\), which never decreases;
- Adamax replaces \(\sqrt{v_t}\) by \(u_t=\max(\beta_2u_{t-1},|g_t|)\);
- Nadam applies a scheduled Nesterov look-ahead to the momentum;
- RMSProp has no momentum and no bias correction;
- Adagrad accumulates \(\sum g_t^2\) without forgetting.

AdaBelief divides by \(s_t=\beta_2s_{t-1}+(1-\beta_2)(g_t-m_t)^2+\epsilon\), the spread of the gradient around its running mean. Steady gradients give a small \(s_t\) and large steps. Yogi updates \(v_t=v_{t-1}-(1-\beta_2)\,\mathrm{sign}(v_{t-1}-g_t^2)\,g_t^2\), which moves \(v\) towards \(g_t^2\) by an amount limited by \(g_t^2\) rather than by \(v\). The denominator therefore cannot collapse as quickly after a burst of large gradients.

>> How large can an Adam step be?

Kingma and Ba note that the step is "approximately bounded" by \(\eta(1-\beta_1)/\sqrt{1-\beta_2}\), which is 3.16 for the defaults, the value of a single large gradient after a long quiet period. The exact bound follows from the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality. Write \(m_t=(1-\beta_1)\sum_ia_ig_i\) and \(v_t=(1-\beta_2)\sum_ib_ig_i^2\) with \(a_i=\beta_1^{t-i}\) and \(b_i=\beta_2^{t-i}\). Then \(|\sum a_ig_i|\le\sqrt{\sum a_i^2/b_i}\sqrt{\sum b_ig_i^2}\), so

$$
\frac{|\hat m_t|}{\sqrt{\hat v_t}}\le\frac{(1-\beta_1)\sqrt{1-\beta_2^t}}{(1-\beta_1^t)\sqrt{1-\beta_2}}\sqrt{\sum_{k=0}^{t-1}\Big(\frac{\beta_1^2}{\beta_2}\Big)^k},
$$
\alttext(The absolute value of m hat t over the square root of v hat t is at most 1 minus beta 1 times the square root of 1 minus beta 2 to the t, over 1 minus beta 1 to the t times the square root of 1 minus beta 2, times the square root of the sum over k from 0 to t minus 1 of beta 1 squared over beta 2 to the k.)

with equality for \(g_i\propto(\beta_1/\beta_2)^{t-i}\), a gradient growing geometrically towards step \(t\). For \(\beta_1^2<\beta_2\) the limit is \((1-\beta_1)/\sqrt{(1-\beta_2)(1-\beta_1^2/\beta_2)}\), which `nn::adam_step_bound` evaluates for any \(t\).

>> RAdam's rectification

Early in training \(v_t\) averages only a few squared gradients, so the adaptive learning rate \(\psi_t=\sqrt{(1-\beta_2^t)/v_t}\) has a large variance. In the first step it is \(1/|g_1|\), whose variance is infinite for Gaussian gradients. Liu et al. approximate \(\psi_t^2\) by a scaled inverse \(\chi^2\) variable with \(\rho_t=\rho_\infty-2t\beta_2^t/(1-\beta_2^t)\) degrees of freedom, \(\rho_\infty=2/(1-\beta_2)-1\). They multiply the step by

$$
r_t=\sqrt{\frac{(\rho_t-4)(\rho_t-2)\rho_\infty}{(\rho_\infty-4)(\rho_\infty-2)\rho_t}}\approx\sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{Var}[\psi_\infty]}{\mathrm{Var}[\psi_t]}},
$$
\alttext(r t equals the square root of rho t minus 4 times rho t minus 2 times rho infinity, over rho infinity minus 4 times rho infinity minus 2 times rho t; approximately the square root of the variance of psi infinity over the variance of psi t.)

and use plain bias-corrected momentum while \(\rho_t\le5\), where the variance does not exist. For \(\beta_2=0.999\) that is the first five steps.

>> Reddi et al.'s counterexample

Reddi, Kale and Kumar (2018) showed that Adam can fail on a convex online problem. On \(x\in[-1,1]\), the loss is \(f_t(x)=Cx\) when \(t\bmod3=1\) and \(-x\) otherwise, with \(C>2\). The average gradient \((C-2)/3\) is positive, so the best fixed point is \(x=-1\).
With \(\beta_1=0\) and \(\beta_2=1/(1+C^2)\), the large gradient is quickly forgotten by \(v_t\), and Adam takes two steps of almost full size towards \(+1\) for every damped step towards \(-1\). It converges to \(x=+1\), the worst point. AMSGrad's non-decreasing denominator prevents this. The regret \(R_T=\sum_tf_t(x_t)-f_t(-1)\) measures the damage: an algorithm that converges to the optimum has \(R_T/T\to0\).

> Simple examples

>> Kernel formulas, first updates, envelopes and a quadratic

The program applies 40 updates of a changing gradient stream to three coordinates and compares Adagrad, RMSProp and Adam with the update formulas of TensorFlow's kernels. It checks every rule's first update from \(w=(1,-2)\) with gradient \((3,-4)\) against its closed form. It follows AMSGrad's and Adamax's state after a single gradient pulse and AdamW's weights under zero gradients.
Finally, it minimises \(L=(w_1-1.5)^2+30(w_2+0.75)^2\) from \((4,-4)\) with every rule for 4000 steps. The learning rate is 0.1, then 0.01 after 3200 steps and 0.001 after 3600, and AdamW uses decay 0.01.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/adaptive_quadratic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Kind = nn::OptimizerKind;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

// One optimizer behind a common interface: the built-in kinds and the AdaptiveOptimizer rules.
struct AnyOpt {
  std::string name;
  std::function<void(const std::vector<Tensor>&)> step;
  std::function<void(double)> set_lr;
};
AnyOpt make(const std::string& which, std::vector<Tensor> params, double lr, double weight_decay = 0.0) {
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, Kind>> builtin{{"Adagrad", Kind::Adagrad}, {"RMSProp", Kind::RMSProp}, {"Adam", Kind::Adam}, {"AdamW", Kind::AdamW},
                                                          {"Nadam", Kind::Nadam},     {"AMSGrad", Kind::AMSGrad}, {"Adamax", Kind::Adamax}};
  for (const auto& [n, k] : builtin)
    if (n == which) {
      nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
      cfg.kind = k;
      if (k == Kind::AdamW) cfg.weight_decay = weight_decay;
      auto o = std::make_shared<nn::Optimizer>(params, lr, cfg);
      return {which, [o](const std::vector<Tensor>& g) { o->step(g); }, [o](double r) { o->set_learning_rate(r); }};
    }
  const auto rule = which == "RAdam" ? nn::AdaptiveRule::RAdam : which == "AdaBelief" ? nn::AdaptiveRule::AdaBelief : nn::AdaptiveRule::Yogi;
  auto o = std::make_shared<nn::AdaptiveOptimizer>(params, lr, nn::AdaptiveConfig{.rule = rule});
  return {which, [o](const std::vector<Tensor>& g) { o->step(g); }, [o](double r) { o->set_learning_rate(r); }};
}
// Draws a polyline clipped to the box [x0, x1] × [y0, y1] (Liang–Barsky on each segment); the label goes on the first piece.
void clipped_lines(dm::graphics::RPlot& pl, const std::vector<double>& x, const std::vector<double>& y, double x0, double x1, double y0, double y1,
                   const std::string& label, RGB color, double width) {
  std::vector<double> cx, cy;
  bool labelled = label.empty();
  auto flush = [&] {
    if (cx.size() >= 2) {
      if (!labelled) pl.lines(cx, cy, label, color, width), labelled = true;
      else pl.lines(cx, cy, color, width);
    }
    cx.clear(), cy.clear();
  };
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i + 1 < x.size(); ++i) {
    const double dx = x[i + 1] - x[i], dy = y[i + 1] - y[i];
    double t0 = 0, t1 = 1;
    bool keep = true;
    for (auto [p, q] : {std::pair{-dx, x[i] - x0}, {dx, x1 - x[i]}, {-dy, y[i] - y0}, {dy, y1 - y[i]}}) {
      if (p == 0) { if (q < 0) keep = false; continue; }
      const double r = q / p;
      if (p < 0) t0 = std::max(t0, r); else t1 = std::min(t1, r);
    }
    if (!keep || t0 > t1) { flush(); continue; }
    const double ax = x[i] + t0 * dx, ay = y[i] + t0 * dy, bx = x[i] + t1 * dx, by = y[i] + t1 * dy;
    if (cx.empty() || cx.back() != ax || cy.back() != ay) { flush(); cx.push_back(ax), cy.push_back(ay); }
    cx.push_back(bx), cy.push_back(by);
    if (t1 < 1) flush();
  }
  flush();
}
const std::vector<std::string> names{"Adagrad", "RMSProp", "Adam", "AdamW", "Nadam", "AMSGrad", "Adamax", "RAdam", "AdaBelief", "Yogi"};
const std::vector<RGB> colors{{148, 163, 184}, {120, 53, 15}, {37, 99, 235}, {14, 165, 233}, {124, 58, 237}, {220, 38, 38}, {202, 138, 4}, {22, 163, 74}, {219, 39, 119}, {17, 24, 39}};

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // 1. Agreement with the fused-kernel formulas of TensorFlow (ApplyAdagradV2, ApplyRMSProp, ApplyAdam) over 40 updates
  // of a changing gradient stream, epsilon 1e-4 so that its placement matters, learning rate 0.03 then 0.01.
  std::print("| rule | max abs(library − kernel formula) over 40 updates |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (Kind kind : {Kind::Adagrad, Kind::RMSProp, Kind::Adam}) {
    Tensor w = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1, -2, 0.5}, {3});
    w.requires_grad_();
    nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
    cfg.kind = kind, cfg.epsilon = 1e-4;
    nn::Optimizer opt({w}, 0.03, cfg);
    std::vector<double> ref{1, -2, 0.5}, first(3, 0.0), second(3, kind == Kind::Adagrad ? 0.1 : 0.0);
    double worst = 0;
    for (int t = 1; t <= 40; ++t) {
      const std::vector<double> g{t % 3 == 0 ? 2.0 : -0.2, t % 4 == 0 ? 0.0 : 0.005, 0.0};
      const double lr = t < 20 ? 0.03 : 0.01;
      opt.set_learning_rate(lr);
      opt.step({nn::from_vector(g, {3})});
      const double b1p = std::pow(cfg.beta1, t), b2p = std::pow(cfg.beta2, t);
      for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        if (kind == Kind::Adagrad) second[i] += g[i] * g[i], ref[i] -= lr * g[i] / (std::sqrt(second[i]) + cfg.epsilon);
        else if (kind == Kind::RMSProp) second[i] += (g[i] * g[i] - second[i]) * (1 - cfg.rho), ref[i] -= lr * g[i] / std::sqrt(second[i] + cfg.epsilon);
        else {
          const double lr_t = lr * std::sqrt(1 - b2p) / (1 - b1p), eps_hat = cfg.epsilon * std::sqrt(1 - b2p);
          first[i] += (g[i] - first[i]) * (1 - cfg.beta1), second[i] += (g[i] * g[i] - second[i]) * (1 - cfg.beta2);
          ref[i] -= lr_t * first[i] / (std::sqrt(second[i]) + eps_hat);
        }
      }
      const auto got = w.to_vector<double>();
      for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(got[i] - ref[i]));
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.1e} |\n", nn::optimizer_name(kind), worst);
  }

  // 2. The first update from gradient (3, −4) at learning rate 0.1, against its closed form.
  std::print("\nFirst update from w = (1, −2) with gradient (3, −4), learning rate 0.1 (AdamW with decay 0.1):\n\n| rule | w after one step | closed form | difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& name : names) {
    Tensor w = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1, -2}, {2});
    w.requires_grad_();
    auto o = make(name, {w}, 0.1, 0.1);
    o.step({nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{3, -4}, {2})});
    std::vector<double> cf(2);
    for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {
      const double g = i == 0 ? 3 : -4, w0 = i == 0 ? 1 : -2;
      double d;
      if (name == "Adagrad") d = g / (std::sqrt(0.1 + g * g) + 1e-8);
      else if (name == "RMSProp") d = g / std::sqrt(0.1 * g * g + 1e-8);
      else if (name == "Nadam") {
        const double mu = 0.9 * (1 - 0.5 * 0.96), mu1 = 0.9 * (1 - 0.5 * 0.96 * 0.96);
        d = (1 + mu1 * 0.1 / (1 - mu * mu1)) * g / (std::abs(g) + 1e-8);
      } else if (name == "RAdam") d = g;  // rho_1 = 1 <= 5: plain bias-corrected momentum
      else if (name == "AdaBelief") d = g / (std::sqrt((0.001 * 0.81 * g * g + 1e-8) / 0.001) + 1e-8);
      else d = g / (std::abs(g) + 1e-8) + (name == "AdamW" ? 0.1 * w0 : 0.0);
      cf[i] = w0 - 0.1 * d;
    }
    const auto got = w.to_vector<double>();
    std::print("| {} | ({:.6f}, {:.6f}) | ({:.6f}, {:.6f}) | {:.1e} |\n", name, got[0], got[1], cf[0], cf[1], std::max(std::abs(got[0] - cf[0]), std::abs(got[1] - cf[1])));
  }

  // 3. Envelopes after a single gradient pulse: AMSGrad's maximum never decreases, Adamax's decays like beta2^t, and
  // AdamW with zero gradients decays the weights geometrically.
  {
    Tensor w = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1, -2}, {2});
    w.requires_grad_();
    nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
    cfg.kind = Kind::AMSGrad;
    nn::Optimizer ams({w}, 0.1, cfg);
    Tensor w2 = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1, -2}, {2});
    w2.requires_grad_();
    cfg.kind = Kind::Adamax;
    nn::Optimizer amax({w2}, 0.1, cfg);
    auto slot = [](const nn::Optimizer& o, const std::string& key) {
      for (const auto& [k, v] : o.state()) if (k == key) return v.to_vector<double>()[0];
      return -1.0;
    };
    const Tensor pulse = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{3, -4}, {2}), zero = nn::zeros({2}, F64);
    ams.step({pulse}), amax.step({pulse});
    double ams_min_ratio = 1e9, amax_err = 0;
    double prev = slot(ams, "vmax/0");
    for (int t = 1; t <= 8; ++t) {
      ams.step({zero}), amax.step({zero});
      ams_min_ratio = std::min(ams_min_ratio, slot(ams, "vmax/0") / prev);
      prev = slot(ams, "vmax/0");
      amax_err = std::max(amax_err, std::abs(slot(amax, "u/0") - 3 * std::pow(0.999, t)));
    }
    Tensor w3 = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1, -2}, {2});
    w3.requires_grad_();
    cfg.kind = Kind::AdamW, cfg.weight_decay = 0.1;
    nn::Optimizer adamw({w3}, 0.1, cfg);
    for (int t = 0; t < 5; ++t) adamw.step({zero});
    std::print("\nAfter a pulse and 8 zero gradients: AMSGrad's maximum changes by a factor of at least {:.6f} per step; Adamax's u differs from 3·0.999^t by at most {:.1e}.\n",
               ams_min_ratio, amax_err);
    std::print("AdamW after 5 zero gradients: w_1 = {:.10f} against (1 − 0.1·0.1)^5 = {:.10f}.\n", w3.to_vector<double>()[0], std::pow(0.99, 5));
  }

  // 4. Every rule on the anisotropic quadratic L = (w1 − 1.5)² + 30 (w2 + 0.75)² from (4, −4), 4000 steps,
  // learning rate 0.1, then 0.01 after 3200 steps and 0.001 after 3600; AdamW with decay 0.01.
  std::print("\nAnisotropic quadratic, minimum at (1.5, −0.75):\n\n| rule | w after 4000 steps | loss | first step with loss < 1e-3 | largest abs(w2 + 0.75) |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  auto paths = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), curves = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  {  // contours of the loss as background ellipses
    for (double level : {1.0, 4.0, 16.0, 64.0}) {
      std::vector<double> cx, cy;
      for (int k = 0; k <= 200; ++k) {
        const double a = 2 * M_PI * k / 200;
        cx.push_back(1.5 + std::sqrt(level) * std::cos(a)), cy.push_back(-0.75 + std::sqrt(level / 30) * std::sin(a));
      }
      clipped_lines(paths, cx, cy, -1, 6.5, -4.5, 4, "", RGB{226, 232, 240}, 1);
    }
  }
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < names.size(); ++r) {
    Tensor w = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{4, -4}, {2});
    w.requires_grad_();
    const Tensor target = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1.5, -0.75}, {2}), weight = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1, 30}, {2});
    auto loss = [&] { return nn::sum(weight * nn::square(w - target)); };
    auto o = make(names[r], {w}, 0.1, 0.01);
    std::vector<double> px{4}, py{-4}, ls, ts;
    int first = -1;
    double far = 0;
    for (int t = 1; t <= 4000; ++t) {
      o.set_lr(t <= 3200 ? 0.1 : t <= 3600 ? 0.01 : 0.001);
      o.step(nn::grad(loss(), {w}));
      const auto v = w.to_vector<double>();
      const double l = loss().item();
      if (t <= 400) px.push_back(v[0]), py.push_back(v[1]);
      if (t % 10 == 0) ts.push_back(t), ls.push_back(std::log10(std::max(l, 1e-16)));
      if (first < 0 && l < 1e-3) first = t;
      far = std::max(far, std::abs(v[1] + 0.75));
    }
    const auto v = w.to_vector<double>();
    std::print("| {} | ({:.5f}, {:.5f}) | {:.2e} | {} | {:.3g} |\n", names[r], v[0], v[1], loss().item(), first < 0 ? std::string("never") : std::to_string(first), far);
    if (names[r] == "Adagrad" || names[r] == "Adam" || names[r] == "AMSGrad" || names[r] == "RAdam" || names[r] == "AdaBelief") clipped_lines(paths, px, py, -1, 6.5, -4.5, 4, names[r], colors[r], 2);
    curves.lines(ts, ls, names[r], colors[r], 1.5);
  }
  paths.x_limits(-1, 6.5).y_limits(-4.5, 4).title("Paths of the first 400 steps").x_label("w1").y_label("w2");
  curves.x_limits(0, 4100).y_limits(-17, 9).title("Loss on the quadratic").x_label("step").y_label("log10 loss");
  fig.add(paths), fig.add(curves);
  fig.size(1100, 520).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/adaptive_quadratic.svg");
}
```
