// result_end

\caption(Top left: the largest Adam step in learning rates, from the Cauchy–Schwarz bound (red; the library's step on the extremal history coincides), a single spike (blue) and 2000 random histories (green). Top right: RAdam's rectification term (red line) against its Monte Carlo target (blue points). Bottom: Reddi et al.'s problem; the mean of the iterate over blocks of 51 steps (left; the losses have period 3) and the average regret (right). Adam and RMSProp coincide here.)
\alttext(Top left: the red bound stays near 1 up to t = 10 and then rises to 7.2 at t = 5000; the blue spike curve dips to 0.5 around t = 10 and rises to 3.15; the green random curve stays between 0.75 and 1. Top right: the blue points lie on or slightly above the red curve, which rises from near 0 at t = 6 to 1 at t = 5000. Bottom left: Adam and RMSProp go to +1 within 100 steps; AMSGrad and Yogi go to −1 almost as fast, Adagrad slowly; AdaBelief is flat at 1/3. Bottom right: AdaBelief's average regret is flat at 1.33, Adam's and RMSProp's at 0.67, and the other three fall towards 0.)

The library's Adam step on the extremal history equals the bound to all four printed digits at every \(t\), from 1.0000 at \(t=1\) through 2.2434 at \(t=100\) to 7.2458 at \(t=5000\). The limit is 7.2703, against Kingma and Ba's 3.1623. A single spike approaches 3.16 from below, and it is not the worst case. Random Gaussian histories never exceed about one learning rate, so the large steps need a gradient that grows steadily over hundreds of steps.

RAdam's \(r_t\) agrees with the Monte Carlo ratio to within 7% from \(t=20\) on: 0.0848 against 0.0910 at \(t=20\), 0.2153 against 0.2212 at 100 and 0.6453 against 0.6849 at 1000. In the first steps after the switch it is smaller than the target: 0.026 against 0.038 at \(t=6\), and 0.069 against 0.076 at \(t=15\). The approximation is conservative, so RAdam steps more cautiously than the variance argument requires.

On Reddi et al.'s problem, Adam and RMSProp, which coincide for \(\beta_1=0\) apart from bias correction, end at \(x=0.999\) with average regret 0.67. AMSGrad, Yogi and Adagrad end within 0.002 of the optimum with average regrets 0.004, 0.007 and 0.009, still falling. Yogi's additive update can lower its denominator by at most \((1-\beta_2)g_t^2=0.9\) per small gradient, so it plausibly keeps the memory of the large gradient that AMSGrad keeps with its maximum.
AdaBelief does worst, and the reason is exact. With \(\beta_1=0\), \(m_t=g_t\), so its belief term \(g_t-m_t\) is identically zero. Its denominator is then \(\sqrt{\epsilon/(1-\beta_2)}\approx10^{-4}\), and every step hits a boundary. The iterate cycles through \(-1,+1,+1\), with mean 1/3, and each cycle costs \(2C-2=4\) for an average regret of 4/3. The run gives 0.3333 and 1.3334. RAdam is not shown: with \(\beta_2=0.1\), \(\rho_\infty=1.22\) never exceeds 5, so RAdam is plain SGD.

> A dataset: ten rules on digits

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) has 1797 handwritten digits of \(8\times8\) pixels, scaled to \([0,1]\). Here the first 1137 train, the next 300 validate and the last 360 test. An MLP with one hidden ReLU layer of 128 units is trained with each rule for 1500 batches of 64 at learning rates 0.001, 0.003, 0.01 and 0.03, for two seeds. The rate with the best mean validation accuracy is chosen, and test accuracy is reported at that rate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/adaptive_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Kind = nn::OptimizerKind;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, n_train = 1137, n_val = 300, n_test = n - n_train - n_val;
  std::vector<float> px(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * 64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto col = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) px[i * 64 + j] = static_cast<float>(col[i] / 16.0);
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(px, {n, 64});
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0}, {n_train, 64}), ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_train});
  const Tensor Xva = nn::slice(X, {n_train, 0}, {n_val, 64}), yva = nn::slice(y, {n_train}, {n_val});
  const Tensor Xte = nn::slice(X, {n_train + n_val, 0}, {n_test, 64}), yte = nn::slice(y, {n_train + n_val}, {n_test});

  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Adagrad", "RMSProp", "Adam", "AdamW", "Nadam", "AMSGrad", "Adamax", "RAdam", "AdaBelief", "Yogi"};
  const std::vector<RGB> colors{{148, 163, 184}, {120, 53, 15}, {37, 99, 235}, {14, 165, 233}, {124, 58, 237}, {220, 38, 38}, {202, 138, 4}, {22, 163, 74}, {219, 39, 119}, {17, 24, 39}};
  const std::vector<double> rates{1e-3, 3e-3, 1e-2, 3e-2};
  const int steps = 1500;
  std::print("MLP 64-128-10 (ReLU), {} training, {} validation and {} test images, {} steps of batch 64, two seeds; AdamW decay 0.01.\n\n", n_train, n_val, n_test, steps);
  std::print("| rule | validation accuracy at η = 0.001 | 0.003 | 0.01 | 0.03 | chosen η | test accuracy at chosen η | final training loss | first step with training loss < 0.1 |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> val(names.size()), best_curve(names.size());
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < names.size(); ++r) {
    double best_val = -1, best_test = 0, best_loss = 0, best_rate = 0;
    for (double rate : rates) {
      double va = 0, te = 0, fl = 0;
      std::vector<double> curve(steps / 50, 0.0);
      for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2}) {
        nn::ParameterStore p(seed, F32);
        auto f = [&](const Tensor& x) {
          return nn::dense(p, "out", nn::dense(p, "h", x, 128, {.activation = nn::Activation::Relu, .init = nn::init::he_normal()}), 10);
        };
        { nn::NoGradGuard ng; (void)f(nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0}, {2, 64})); }
        std::function<void(const Tensor&)> minimize;
        std::shared_ptr<nn::Optimizer> o1;
        std::shared_ptr<nn::AdaptiveOptimizer> o2;
        if (names[r] == "RAdam" || names[r] == "AdaBelief" || names[r] == "Yogi") {
          const auto rule = names[r] == "RAdam" ? nn::AdaptiveRule::RAdam : names[r] == "AdaBelief" ? nn::AdaptiveRule::AdaBelief : nn::AdaptiveRule::Yogi;
          o2 = std::make_shared<nn::AdaptiveOptimizer>(p, rate, nn::AdaptiveConfig{.rule = rule});
          minimize = [&](const Tensor& l) { o2->minimize(l); };
        } else {
          nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
          const std::vector<Kind> kinds{Kind::Adagrad, Kind::RMSProp, Kind::Adam, Kind::AdamW, Kind::Nadam, Kind::AMSGrad, Kind::Adamax};
          cfg.kind = kinds[r];
          if (cfg.kind == Kind::AdamW) cfg.weight_decay = 0.01;
          o1 = std::make_shared<nn::Optimizer>(p, rate, cfg);
          minimize = [&](const Tensor& l) { o1->minimize(l); };
        }
        std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
        std::vector<std::int64_t> order(n_train);
        std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
        std::size_t pos = n_train;
        double running = 0;
        for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) {
          if (pos + 64 > order.size()) std::shuffle(order.begin(), order.end(), rng), pos = 0;
          const Tensor idx = nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>(order.begin() + pos, order.begin() + pos + 64), {64});
          pos += 64;
          const Tensor loss = nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(nn::gather(Xtr, idx, 0)), nn::gather(ytr, idx, 0));
          running += loss.item() / 50;
          minimize(loss);
          if (s % 50 == 0) curve[s / 50 - 1] += running / 2, running = 0;
        }
        nn::NoGradGuard ng;
        auto acc = [&](const Tensor& Xs, const Tensor& ys) { return nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(f(Xs), -1), ys), F32)).item(); };
        va += acc(Xva, yva) / 2, te += acc(Xte, yte) / 2;
        fl += nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(Xtr), ytr).item() / 2;
      }
      val[r].push_back(va);
      if (va > best_val) best_val = va, best_test = te, best_loss = fl, best_rate = rate, best_curve[r] = curve;
    }
    std::print("| {} |", names[r]);
    for (double v : val[r]) std::print(" {:.1f}% |", 100 * v);
    int first = -1;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < best_curve[r].size() && first < 0; ++k) if (best_curve[r][k] < 0.1) first = 50 * static_cast<int>(k + 1);
    std::print(" {} | {:.1f}% | {:.4f} | {} |\n", best_rate, 100 * best_test, best_loss, first);
  }

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> xs;
    for (double rate : rates) xs.push_back(std::log10(rate));
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < names.size(); ++r) {
      std::vector<double> ys;
      for (double v : val[r]) ys.push_back(100 * v);
      pl.lines(xs, ys, names[r], colors[r], 2).points(xs, ys, colors[r], 5);
    }
    pl.x_limits(-3.1, -1.4).x_ticks({-3, std::log10(3e-3), -2, std::log10(3e-2)}, {"0.001", "0.003", "0.01", "0.03"}).y_limits(70, 145)
        .y_ticks({70, 75, 80, 85, 90, 95, 100}, {"70", "75", "80", "85", "90", "95", "100"})
        .title("Validation accuracy by learning rate").x_label("learning rate η (log scale)").y_label("validation accuracy (%)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < names.size(); ++r) {
      std::vector<double> xs, ys;
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < best_curve[r].size(); ++k) xs.push_back(50.0 * (k + 1)), ys.push_back(std::log10(best_curve[r][k]));
      pl.lines(xs, ys, names[r], colors[r], 1.5);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, steps * 1.02).y_limits(-4.5, 2.2).title("Training loss at the chosen rate").x_label("training step").y_label("log10 cross-entropy (mean of 50 batches)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 540).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/adaptive_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
MLP 64-128-10 (ReLU), 1137 training, 300 validation and 360 test images, 1500 steps of batch 64, two seeds; AdamW decay 0.01.

| rule | validation accuracy at η = 0.001 | 0.003 | 0.01 | 0.03 | chosen η | test accuracy at chosen η | final training loss | first step with training loss < 0.1 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Adagrad | 74.0% | 92.0% | 94.2% | 96.0% | 0.03 | 90.0% | 0.0475 | 700 |
| RMSProp | 96.8% | 97.2% | 97.0% | 97.0% | 0.003 | 90.7% | 0.0001 | 200 |
| Adam | 96.8% | 96.8% | 96.8% | 96.8% | 0.001 | 90.6% | 0.0146 | 500 |
| AdamW | 97.0% | 96.8% | 96.8% | 96.8% | 0.001 | 90.6% | 0.0151 | 500 |
| Nadam | 96.8% | 97.0% | 96.8% | 97.2% | 0.03 | 91.4% | 0.0001 | 100 |
| AMSGrad | 96.8% | 96.8% | 96.8% | 97.0% | 0.03 | 90.7% | 0.0001 | 100 |
| Adamax | 96.5% | 96.7% | 96.7% | 96.8% | 0.03 | 90.3% | 0.0006 | 100 |
| RAdam | 96.2% | 96.3% | 96.8% | 97.3% | 0.03 | 90.8% | 0.0002 | 150 |
| AdaBelief | 97.0% | 96.7% | 96.7% | 97.2% | 0.03 | 90.8% | 0.0002 | 100 |
| Yogi | 96.7% | 96.8% | 97.0% | 97.3% | 0.03 | 90.8% | 0.0001 | 100 |
\image(figures/adaptive_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: validation accuracy (mean of two seeds) of each rule against the learning rate. Right: training loss at each rule's chosen rate (mean over 50 batches, log scale).)
\alttext(Left: nine rules lie in a flat band between 96 and 97.5% at every learning rate; Adagrad's grey line rises from 74% at 0.001 to 96% at 0.03. Right: Nadam falls fastest, to 10⁻⁴·² by step 1500; most rules end between 10⁻³·⁵ and 10⁻⁴; Adamax ends near 10⁻³·², Adam and AdamW, chosen at 0.001, near 10⁻¹·⁸, and Adagrad near 10⁻¹·³.)

At their best learning rates all ten rules reach 96.0–97.3% validation accuracy and 90.0–91.4% test accuracy. With 300 validation images, one image is 0.33 points and the standard error of an accuracy near 97% is about 1 point, so these differences are noise. Test accuracy is lower than validation accuracy for every rule, which suggests the last 360 images are harder than the rest. That affects all rules equally.
The rules differ in speed and sensitivity. Nine of them are within 1.1 points of each other across the whole 30-fold range of learning rates; only Adagrad needs the largest rate, reaching 74% at 0.001. At the chosen rate, the training loss first drops below 0.1 after 100 steps for Nadam, AMSGrad, Adamax, AdaBelief and Yogi, after 150 for RAdam, 200 for RMSProp, 500 for Adam and AdamW (chosen at 0.001) and 700 for Adagrad.

> Conclusion

The built-in adaptive optimizers implement their published update rules exactly, and the three added rules match their definitions to \(10^{-12}\). The analysis gives three sharper statements than the usual ones. An Adam step can reach 7.27 learning rates at the default settings, but only for a gradient that grows steadily for hundreds of steps; random gradients stay near one. RAdam's rectification is a slightly conservative estimate of the variance ratio it targets, and its non-adaptive start can itself be unstable. On Reddi et al.'s problem, the fix for Adam's non-convergence is a denominator that does not forget, AMSGrad's maximum or Yogi's additive update. AdaBelief with \(\beta_1=0\) degenerates to sign descent with huge steps.

On a small, easy classification task none of this changes the final accuracy: every rule reached the same validation accuracy within noise. The rules differed in how quickly they reduced the training loss and in how sensitive they were to the learning rate, and Adagrad stood apart in needing a larger rate.

